   "source": [
    "# AI Bias Auditing Framework for Labour Market Disparity Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "**CCT College Dublin | BSc Computing in IT | Final Capstone Project (CA2)**  \n",
    "**Student:** Ana Clara R. Quilapan - 2022475  \n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook implements an end-to-end AI bias auditing framework following the **CRISP-DM methodology**, using the American Community Survey (ACS) Public Use Microdata Sample (PUMS) for Florida.  \n",
    "\n",
    "### Project Objective  \n",
    "\n",
    "To identify and analyse demographic disparities in labour market outcomes through machine learning models, and to demonstrate how such outcomes can be audited using fairness analysis, governance principles, and an auditable MySQL-based compliance layer.  \n",
    "\n",
    "### Dataset  \n",
    "\n",
    "**ACS PUMS Florida Dataset**  \n",
    "\n",
    "- Population: Working-age individuals (18–66 years)  \n",
    "- Records analysed: 126,430  \n",
    "- Focus: Employment outcomes and demographic disparities  \n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "The notebook covers the complete project workflow, including:  \n",
    "\n",
    "- Data Understanding\n",
    "- Data Preparation\n",
    "- Machine Learning Modelling\n",
    "- Fairness and Disparity Analysis\n",
    "- Model Evaluation\n",
    "- MySQL Audit Storage and Compliance Reporting"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7898be7e-574b-41cd-8e35-ec21b0f8e4a6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 1. Business Understanding\n",
    "\n",
    "Recruitment is a high-impact decision-making context because automated screening or ranking tools can influence who progresses to interviews or employment opportunities. Even when an AI system is not intentionally discriminatory, its outputs may still differ across demographic groups due to historical, structural or data-related patterns.\n",
    "\n",
    "For this reason, the project focuses on auditing rather than automated hiring. The audit framework treats model outputs as evidence that should be reviewed by humans, not as final hiring decisions. This supports responsible AI principles such as transparency, accountability and human oversight (European Commission High-Level Expert Group on AI, 2019; NIST, 2023)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3973db8d-16fb-4674-95a8-c0c04c6f20db",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## CRISP-DM Structure Used in This Notebook\n",
    "\n",
    "The notebook is organised according to the six CRISP-DM phases (Chapman et al., 2000):\n",
    "\n",
    "| CRISP-DM Phase | How it is addressed in this notebook |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Business Understanding | Project aim, research question and governance problem definition |\n",
    "| Data Understanding | Dataset inspection, quality checks and exploratory visualisations |\n",
    "| Data Preparation | Filtering, target creation, leakage prevention, encoding and train/test split |\n",
    "| Modelling | Baseline model comparison, Logistic Regression, Random Forest and tuned Random Forest |\n",
    "| Evaluation | Classification metrics, ROC-AUC, k-fold cross-validation and model comparison |\n",
    "| Deployment | MySQL audit database, prediction logging, fairness logging and compliance queries |\n",
    "\n",
    "This structure is used to ensure that the notebook is not only code-based, but also demonstrates a logical project methodology and decision-making process."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d62373fe-9aed-445f-9a81-868cd2b85567",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Research Question\n",
    "\n",
    "**Can an AI auditing framework identify and monitor potential disparities in recruitment-related decision outcomes across demographic groups while maintaining transparency, reproducibility and auditability (Mehrabi et al., 2021; Ahmad et al., 2025)?**\n",
    "\n",
    "This research question is addressed through four technical objectives:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. prepare a structured dataset suitable for employment-style modelling;\n",
    "2. train and compare machine learning models using appropriate validation methods;\n",
    "3. evaluate subgroup-level performance differences across sex, disability and race-related categories;\n",
    "4. record predictions, fairness metrics and audit summaries in a relational database."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "833233df-f82e-43a6-a520-58ba3185e731",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Project Aim\n",
    "\n",
    "The aim of this project is to design and implement an AI bias auditing framework for recruitment decision support. The framework trains machine learning models on a public employment-style dataset, evaluates predictive performance, analyses subgroup-level fairness metrics and stores audit outputs in a MySQL database for traceability.\n",
    "\n",
    "The intended business value is not simply prediction accuracy. The main value is governance: making model behaviour visible, measurable and auditable (Murikah et al., 2024) so that HR, compliance or management stakeholders can review potential disparities before relying on AI-assisted recruitment decisions."
   ]
  },
